An earlier revision of this file said that "anyone redistributing this build
should read the upstream licence before shipping it". That is advice. Advice
discharges an obligation under no licence at all, and repeating it here would
have made this file look like compliance while changing nothing. What follows
is the obligation itself and the decision that has not been made.

WHO OWES WHAT
-------------

An end user who merely runs the hosted app is a USER of the AGPL, not a
conveyer. Running it is not distribution, so the everyday visitor owes
nothing and this section is not addressed to them.

Anyone who DISTRIBUTES this build is conveying a combined work — this
application plus the AGPL library linked into it — and owes the corresponding
source under AGPL section 5(a). Section 13 additionally brings a hosted
MODIFIED deployment into scope, so "it only runs on GitHub Pages" is a
distribution question rather than a way out of one. The library's own weights
are fetched from imgly's CDN at runtime and are not in this repository; that
narrows what is bundled, not what is owed for the bundle.

THE THREE OPTIONS, AND WHAT EACH COSTS
--------------------------------------

This repository does not choose between them. The choice is an owner's
decision about licensing, hosting and product scope, and it belongs to
somebody who can answer those questions. It is recorded here, and rendered on
`/licenses.html`, so that it cannot be lost by being unwritten.

  1. OBTAIN PERMISSION. Ask imgly for a commercial licence, for the cut-out to
     be relicensed, or for an explicit exemption. It may be granted and it may
     not, on their timetable rather than ours, and until it is in writing the
     obligation stands. Cost: an unknown amount of waiting, and no guarantee.
     Nothing else in the tree has to change while the request is outstanding.

  2. REPLACE THE DEPENDENCY. Implement or license background removal another
     way and drop the package. This is the only option that ends the
     obligation by removing its cause. Cost: a matting model and a runtime, and
     the manual background-replacement path that already exists is not a
     cut-out — it needs a matte to show through, and the matte is what this
     library produces. The Background tool cannot ship in its current form
     without it.

  3. PUBLISH THE CORRESPONDING SOURCE. Oblige under section 5(a) rather than
     asking for or avoiding anything: release this application's complete
     corresponding source alongside every distribution, including the build
     scripts and the modifications a recipient makes. This project's own code
     is MIT and stays MIT; the AGPL reaches only the combined work. Cost: an
     ongoing obligation, and the loss of the ability to distribute the build as
     a closed product.

Until one of those is chosen, the accurate statement is the one above: the
obligation exists and is unmet for anyone who conveys this build.

TWO OBLIGATIONS THAT ARE NOT "ADD A CREDIT LINE"
-----------------------------------------------

1. Thirteen of the fourteen bundled fonts are OFL 1.1.

   Each shipped woff2 is the latin or latin-ext subset Google Fonts published,
   copied byte-for-byte and verified against the sha256 pinned in
   `models.lock.json`. Nothing here is subset, repacked or renamed by this
   project. Two clauses of OFL 1.1 follow from that:

     - Section 2 requires a copy of the licence to accompany every copy of the
       Font Software. `public/fonts/OFL-1.1.txt` is that copy, and it sits
       beside the woff2 files rather than behind a link. The one Apache-2.0
       font, Roboto Mono, is covered the same way by
       `public/fonts/Apache-2.0.txt` (Apache-2.0 section 4(a)).

     - Section 3 forbids a Modified Version from using the Reserved Font Name.
       It is not engaged here, and it is worth being precise about why: no
       Modified Version is created. The files are Google's own published
       subsets, unmodified, so the names in them are Google's to use. An
       earlier revision of this file said a subset is a Modified Version and
       that the `IE <Family>` rename is what satisfies section 3; that
       contradicted the bytes and the same paragraph below it.

   The Text panel lists the faces as `IE Inter`, `IE Playfair` and so on. That
   is the CSS family alias `src/features/layers/fonts.ts` registers, so a
   machine with Inter installed cannot win the canvas; it is not a rename of the
   file, whose internal name table still carries the family Google published.

WHAT IS NOT LISTED
------------------

`devDependencies` are not distributed to a browser and have no entry. The
transitive packages that a `dependencies` entry drags in are listed, because
they do ship: `jspdf` lazily imports `html2canvas` and `dompurify`, and reaches
for `canvg` on `globalThis`, so the first two become separate chunks of the
build. One of those, `comlink`, is declared as a runtime dependency and
imported by nothing in `src/`, so it contributes nothing to the bundle; it is
listed because the table records what `package.json` claims and the gate checks
that claim in both directions.

The sample photographs reachable from the import screen are credited on that
screen. They are not bundled in this repository and are not part of this notice.
